.TH iron-vault-import 1  "Iron Vault" "Iron Vault Manual"
.SH NAME
iron\-vault\-import \- Make the vault from a migration bundle that `export` wrote on another machine. The service prompts for the export passphrase, shows a summary, prompts for a new vault passphrase, shows a new recovery key once on the controlling terminal and, once you confirm you saved it, writes the vault, the source\*(Aqs credentials and approval counters included. It needs a controlling terminal, and leaves the service Locked. The vault routes nothing until you approve a config with `config approve`, at a version above every one the source reserved. The BUNDLE must be a regular file of at most 260 MiB
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBiron\-vault import\fR [\fB\-\-admin\-socket\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIBUNDLE\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Make the vault from a migration bundle that `export` wrote on another machine. The service prompts for the export passphrase, shows a summary, prompts for a new vault passphrase, shows a new recovery key once on the controlling terminal and, once you confirm you saved it, writes the vault, the source\*(Aqs credentials and approval counters included. It needs a controlling terminal, and leaves the service Locked. The vault routes nothing until you approve a config with `config approve`, at a version above every one the source reserved. The BUNDLE must be a regular file of at most 260 MiB
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-admin\-socket\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Use the admin socket at PATH instead of this installation\*(Aqs, for example one forwarded over SSH. Every client command takes it; `serve` refuses it, because the service always binds its own installation\*(Aqs socket
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
<\fIBUNDLE\fR>
The bundle file to read
